    sections: ["diagnostics", "early-detection"], status: "emerging", since: 2012,
    tldr: "Tests that read RNA from blood or urine, carried in tiny membrane packets, platelets or protein complexes, to find a cancer or read what its cells are doing.",
    summary: "A tumour's RNA reaches blood and urine inside extracellular vesicles, bound to proteins, or taken up by platelets, and tests can read it there. Several urine tests are in routine use: ExoDx Prostate (three genes from urine vesicles), SelectMDx, MyProstateScore 2.0 and the Xpert Bladder Cancer Monitor, and Medicare's prostate biomarker policy lists the first three. In blood the field is younger: GRAIL's cell-free transcriptome study found that 68 per cent of 57,820 annotated genes are not detected in plasma from people without cancer, so tumour transcripts in those 'dark channels' stand out against a quiet background (Larson 2021); Exai Bio's orphan non-coding RNAs detected lung and colorectal cancer in validation sets (Karimzadeh 2024, Momen-Roknabadi 2025); and platelet RNA sequencing separated cancer from health in research cohorts (Best 2015).\n\nThe limits are measured. Naked RNA does not survive in plasma: more than 99 per cent of added free RNA could no longer be amplified after 15 seconds, while the body's own plasma RNA was stable for 24 hours in blood kept at 4 degrees (Tsui 2002), which is why what survives is packaged. Most of it comes from blood cells: 74 per cent (plus or minus 10) of circulating transcripts in people without cancer were from blood cells (Larson 2021, full text). No published study states a tumour fraction for cell-free RNA in parts per million, the unit residual disease DNA tests use, and no reference material for cell-free RNA assays was found.",
    principle: "RNA is extracted from plasma, urine, isolated vesicles or platelets, converted to DNA and measured by reverse-transcription PCR, targeted panels, microarrays or sequencing; a classifier turns the pattern into a score, or a tumour-specific transcript or fusion is called directly.",
    strengths: ["Reads which genes are switched on, including splice forms and fusions that DNA tests miss", "Some tumour transcripts are absent from normal plasma, so background is low", "Urine tests avoid a biopsy for the decision they inform"],
    limitations: ["Free RNA is destroyed within seconds in plasma; only packaged RNA survives", "Most plasma RNA comes from blood cells, so expression signals need tumour-specific genes", "No parts-per-million tumour fraction or shared reference material for cell-free RNA", "Blood tests for cancer detection are research or early commercial products"],
